ref 17592186045417] [17592186169159 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "355cb361-2b35-5c63-afc5-b2b40c97f410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169160 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169160 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169160 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7d31f461-f500-5aca-80bc-de96d84f3b66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169161 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169161 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169161 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169161 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186169161 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "58d10346-2f0e-5ef5-8bca-1a94b1b9fdae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171716 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171716 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z) =  x-y-z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=2$ and the cylinder $x^2+z^2=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171716 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbe81109-2a56-5dd3-bab5-4ac5cc6ca860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171716 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) =   x-y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2 + z^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -1=\\lambda  \\\\ -1=\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-1 \\\\ \\mu z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu$ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $z= 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ 2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ 2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ -2+y+0 & =2 \\\\ -2+y & =2 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2, 0, 0), \\qquad (-2, 4, 0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2, 0, 0 )$ | $(-2, 4, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $2$ | $-6$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2, 4, 0),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171717 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12459" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171717 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we want to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=2xz+y^2$ on the intersection of the surfaces $x-z=2$ and $x^2+y^2+2z^2=3$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2z,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2x⟩=\\lambda ⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ x-z=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171717 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8496dce-3f8b-5516-874d-17d82cfb41b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171717 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 2xz+y^2\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2+2z^2=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2z,\\,2y,\\,2x⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,4z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are $2y,\\:$ $-1,\\:$ and $4z.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a Lagrange System With Two Constraints" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172134 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "496fa617-6592-57af-89ac-b77d68df0bb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172135 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "355c5dfd-d06f-56ef-9c38-8dccf702467e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172187 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172187 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172187 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172187 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172187 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172187 #edb/ref 1045 "Lagrange Multipliers With Multiple Constraints" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172187 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186173090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172187 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172187 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172187 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "726b9aec-d97b-56fa-bd36-a4d677e8f34f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172382 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172382 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172382 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172382 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x-2y+z$ on the intersection of the plane $x-y+z=1$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172382 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "332ef138-daaa-584b-88e1-280c181c6e29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172382 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x-2y+z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -2=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+z & =1 \\\\ 1-(-1)+z & =1 \\\\ 2+z & =1 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+z & =1 \\\\ -1-1+z & =1 \\\\ -2+z & =1 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-1,-1),\\,(-1,1,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,-1,-1)$ | $(-1,1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,1,3),$ and its minimum value is $-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172383 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172383 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172383 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172383 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}z$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1$ and ${y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨{y}^{2}z,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,x{y}^{2}⟩=\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172383 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7b3533a-918e-5b93-aa55-224813aed39b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172383 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨{y}^{2}z,\\,2xyz,\\,x{y}^{2}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,-4z⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,9{z}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173090 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173090 #edb/ref 1061 44838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173090 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf9461c1-00f3-5778-a2ae-8c56f71f3522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173090 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173091 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173091 #edb/ref 1061 44839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173091 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e24f68c-7b46-5dbe-8a10-39bed7e5ffb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173091 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173092 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173092 #edb/ref 1061 44841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173092 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6f4656de-85c7-583b-9753-5e82a5f0e76f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173092 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173096 #edb/ref 1061 45619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173096 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "db6afef3-511c-5ea5-8a1f-0ce1b0640983" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173096 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173258 #edb/ref 1064 "We can find global extrema of multivariable functions subject to multiple constraints using the method of Lagrange multipliers. \n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 4xy+z^2\n$$\n\nsubject to the following *two* constraints: \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2=1, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0\n$$\n\nGeometrically, this means that we want to minimize $f(x,y,z)$ over the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b16f73e034724a9f38d91a606f627db85299507552c45192e91c39bc0c6725a.png)\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that the extrema of the function $f(x,y,z)$ subject to constraints $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b$ can be found by solving the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=a \\\\ h(x,y,z)=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the constants $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ are the Lagrange multipliers of this system.\n\nAs before, this method assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\nabla h \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curves $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b,$ and that the extreme values exist.\n\n**Note:** Geometrically, the condition $\\nabla f = \\lambda \\nabla g + \\mu \\nabla h$ states that\n\n- $\\nabla f$ lies in the plane spanned by $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h,$ or, equivalently,\n\n- $\\nabla f$ is perpendicular to the level surfaces $g(x,y,z) = a$ and $h(x,y,z) = b.$\n\nWe'll explore this geometric intuition further at the end of the lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173258 #edb/ref 1065 7745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173258 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a4ed8aa-0e0e-50d4-9745-3d68b92cca3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173258 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173262 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now justify why the Lagrange multipliers method works with multiple constraints.\n\nSuppose we wish to optimize the function $f(x,y,z)$ subject to the constraints $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b.$ \n\nNote the following:\n\n- The natural domain of $f$ is some subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- If we were to impose the first constraint only, we'd restrict the domain of $f$ to points on the level surface $g(x,y,z)=a.$\n\n- Similarly, if we were to impose the second constraint only, we'd restrict the domain of $f$ to points on the level surface $h(x,y,z)=b.$\n\n- Thus, by imposing *both* constraints, we restrict the domain of $f$ to the *intersection* of these two level surfaces. The intersection of two surfaces is a curve, which we'll denote as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some real parameter $t.$\n\nThe curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ and the level surfaces are shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8b1db73c9952642ba53983ffb7090ad147ae965f1b935a812811742330b4dfc.png)\n\nNow, let $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ be a point that lies on $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some $t = t_0$ that is an extrema (max or min) of $f.$\n\nSince $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b$ are level surfaces of the functions $g(x,y,z)$ and $h(x,y,z),$ we have the following:\n\n- $\\nabla g$ is perpendicular to $g(x,y,z)=a$ at $P,$ and is therefore perpendicular to $\\mathbf r(t)$ at $P.$\n\n- $\\nabla h$ is perpendicular to $h(x,y,z)=b$ at $P,$ and is therefore perpendicular to $\\mathbf r(t)$ at $P.$\n\nThe situation is sketched below.\n\n![](images/16/16f2f1818628d9eb12ed0f3f2834cd916101839362813afe1be3f90ea35dd104.png)\n\nLet's now compute $\\big[f(\\mathbf r(t))\\big]',$ the derivative of $f$ over the curve $\\mathbf r(t).$ By the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[f(r(t))]^{′} & =[f(x(t),y(t),z(t))]^{′} \\\\  & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\cdot {x}^{′}(t)+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\cdot {y}^{′}(t)+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\cdot {z}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩\\cdot ⟨{x}^{′}(t),{y}^{′}(t),{z}^{′}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ is an extremum, we must have \n\n$$\n\\big[f(\\mathbf{r}(t_0))\\big]' = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t_0)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t_0) = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t_0)) \\perp \\mathbf{r}'(t_0).\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}'(t_0)$ is tangent to $\\mathbf r(t)$ at $P,$ we have that $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t_0))$ is perpendicular to $\\mathbf r(t)$ at $P.$\n\nSo, $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h$ are both perpendicular to $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $P$. Assuming they aren't parallel, they span the two-dimensional space perpendicular to $\\mathbf{r}(t)$. Now $\\nabla f$ is also perpendicular to $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $P$. This means that $\\nabla f$ lies in the plane spanned by $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h.$ This condition can be represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z) + \\mu \\nabla h(x,y,z),\n$$\n\nwhere the real numbers $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ are the Lagrange multipliers.\n\n![](images/b4/b48d7973b28bbd6a99704736dd60737d2f74ebcc2ddfddb0ab6f4749002e826e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173262 #edb/ref 1065 7919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173262 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "669fb037-8c12-5658-a176-7753a99e8c7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173262 #edb/ref 1067 "Justification of the Lagrange Multiplier Method With Multiple Constraints" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429054 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429054 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single